--- MultiverseModalForcing.lean
+++ MultiverseModalForcing.lean
@@ -502,4 +502,24 @@
         (.conj (MSentence.dia p) (MSentence.dia (.neg p))) :=
   (contingent_iff_independent w' p).2 ((contingent_iff_independent w p).1 h)
 
+-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
+-- Hypothesis: reflexivity, transitivity, and directedness isolate the modal
+-- principles T, 4, and .2 of forcing, while asymmetric accessibility separates
+-- this logic from S5 and total accessibility identifies contingency with global
+-- multiverse independence.
+-- Experiment: abstract Kripke laws were tested against finite-flip, sink, and
+-- full-accessibility frames; CH was represented by a distinguished atomic claim
+-- whose truth changes between explicit worlds.
+-- Analysis: directedness supplies a common future and therefore .2, but it does
+-- not provide return paths.  This asymmetry explains the failure of the S5
+-- principle B in the sink frame.  Under total accessibility, local possibility
+-- instead collapses to existence somewhere in the multiverse.
+-- Critique: the explicit worlds are propositional semantic models, not internal
+-- models of ZFC, and so establish modal frame consequences rather than relative
+-- consistency or forcing-independence results for ZFC itself.
+-- Synthesis: the general soundness hierarchy, the asymmetric countermodel, and
+-- the contingency–independence bridge jointly distinguish local forcing
+-- modality from global multiverse truth.
+-- !-- End Lab Notes -- !--
+
 
